    "body": "2026 SEMA Show • 2027 Toyota Crown Signia Gets GT-S Concept Treatment for SEMA, Becomes a Sleek Wagon\nSomeone Paid $2.3 Million for a Ferrari Daytona SP3, Drove 80 Miles, Sold for $18.5 Million\n29 photos\nPhoto: Broad Arrow Auctions\nA Ferrari Daytona SP3 just sold for a mind-blowing $18,540,000 just two years after its original owner had paid $2.3 million to drive it off the showroom floor. He obviously bought it as an investment, hoping to cash in one day, but what it returned must have surpassed his expectations by far.\nWhen Ferrari launched the Daytona SP3 , it carried a base sticker price of $2,250,000 in the United States. For this specific example, the original owner paid $2,334,048. So how in the world did it end up as a 794% return on investment in just two years?\nFinished in the show-stopping Verde Franesca, chassis number 314846, was originally ordered through Ferrari North America in Englewood, New Jersey, and delivered directly to the owner via Ferrari of Long Island, which means the first owner must have been based somewhere in the New York metropolitan area.\nThe green Ferrari Daytona SP3's official paperwork and special-series \"Yellow Book\" record its build completion date as October 2024, which means it must have been delivered either in late 2024 or early 2025. The car was sold exactly two years after it rolled off the production line in Maranello, Italy.\nPhoto: Broad Arrow Auctions At the second annual Broad Arrow Auctions Las Vegas Sale, which took place at the luxury Wynn Las Vegas resort, the Daytona sold for a $18,540,000 gross sale price, including the buyer's premium, after the hammer dropped at exactly $16,850,000, setting the ultimate world record price for a non-charity production example of the Ferrari Daytona SP3.\nIn Vegas, bidding escalated quickly in million-dollar offers with two primary bidders being at the forefront of the battle, who advanced from $25,000 to $25,000, holding their ground avidly as the auction crept all the way to $15,850,000. Reports from the scene reveal a controversial moment, with an underbidder claiming their last-second bid was missed.\nWith the room going crazy, the auctioneer made the exceptionally rare, high-stakes decision to cancel the hammer price and reopen bidding at $16,660,000. The bidders pushed the price up another quarter-million dollars, and the car was definitely hammered at $16,850,000.\nSo what is special about the world’s second most expensive Ferrari SP3 Daytona? Unlike most owners who prefer to order their Ferraris in Rosso Corsa, Rosso Magma , which was also the launch color for the model, Giallo Modena, or Nero Daytona, this one is finished in the ultra-rare Verde Franesca.\nPhoto: Broad Arrow Auctions It is a light metallic sage green selected through the Tailor Made program and acquired for a $42,185 premium, which is basically BMW X1 or Mercedes-Benz GLA money. The car sports a glossy removable carbon fiber targa hardtop, matching the lower bodywork trims, front splitter, side sills, and the rear decklid. Custom airbrushed Scuderia Ferrari shields show up on the fenders.\nIt rides on the factory five-spoke forged lightweight alloy wheels, hiding the gloss-black brake calipers, with Pirelli P Zero Corsa high-performance tires. The cabin takes exclusivity one step further, with an incredibly unique two-tone combination of Nero Alcantara and Mustard Aunde Jeans denim fabric.\nAt the moment of the auction, the one-owner Ferrari Daytona SP3 supercar came out of an elite private collection as what was basically a brand-new model, with the odometer displaying only 81 miles (130 kilometers).\nThis means that the owner didn't really enjoy what the mid-rear, transversely mounted 6.5-liter V12 with naturally aspirated firepower can do. Redlining at 9,500 rpm, the V12 pushes out 829 horsepower (840 metric horsepower) and 514 pound-feet (697 Newton meters) of torque.\nPhoto: Broad Arrow Auctions That is enough to make it flash from 0 to 60 mph (0 to 97 kph) in 2.75 seconds, hit 124 mph (200 kph) in 7.4 seconds, and max out at over 211 mph (340 kph). At launch, it was the most powerful un-electrified, pure internal combustion road engine that Ferrari had ever engineered. A seven-speed dual-clutch automatic transmission puts the power down through the rear wheel.\nFor over $18 million, the buyer is going home with the Yellow Book, Ferrari’s special-series certification, matching custom red leather luggage, driving gloves, and a highly detailed 1:8 scale Amalgam model, precisely mimicking the car’s silhouette with the swept panoramic windshield, dramatic butterfly doors, and prominent rear horizontal slats.\nFerrari is building only 599+1 Daytona SP3s, which makes the model exceptionally rare. That is probably why the price on the used car market has gone through the roof. Just under three months prior to the Las Vegas Auction, a yellow Ferrari Daytona SP3 sold for $17,825,000 at RM Sotheby's Monterey Car Week back in August.\nThe only SP3 to ever command more than the one finished in Verde Franesca was a one-off two-tone example finished in Giallo Modena (what did I tell you about paint colors?) and exposed carbon fiber that sold for $26,000,000 at RM Sotheby's Monterey Car Week last year. However, that was a pure charity example built specifically to benefit the Ferrari Foundation.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nFerrari Daytona SP3 2025 Ferrari Daytona SP3 Ferrari Daytona SP3 price Ferrari V12 Ferrari auction\nAbout the author: Elena Luchian\nElena started off as a sports reporter and five years later switched to automotive. How does that happen? Lots of writing, testdrives, traveling, off-road driving courses, track driving courses, defensive driving courses and diplomas. The diplomas might be \"useless\", but the knowledge she gained is priceless. Full profile\nUP NEXT One-Year Wonder: 1975 Plymouth Road Runner Marked the End of an Era",
